(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Modern Object-Oriented Path Management using the pathlib Module \(PEP 428\). Compare pathlib.Path with legacy os.path.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Motivation for pathlib \(PEP 428\):) Tj
T*
(   In legacy Python, filesystem paths were represented as raw strings manipulated via disparate) Tj
T*
(   functions scattered across os, os.path, glob, and shutil. This led to cumbersome, error-prone) Tj
T*
(   string operations.) Tj
T*
(   Introduced in Python 3.4, pathlib encapsulates filesystem paths as rich first-class objects) Tj
T*
(   \(Path\):) Tj
T*
(   - Operator Overloading: The division operator \(/\) is overloaded for intuitive,) Tj
T*
(   platform-independent path concatenation: path = Path\('base'\) / 'sub' / 'file.txt'.) Tj
T*
(   - Fluent API: Reading, writing, checking existence, and finding extensions are methods on the) Tj
T*
(   Path object itself.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   from pathlib import Path) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   def pathlib_modern_demo\(\):) Tj
T*
(   # 1. Instantiate Path object) Tj
T*
(   sandbox = Path\("modern_sandbox"\)) Tj
T*
(   sub_dir = sandbox / "config" / "json" # Intuitive / operator!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Create directory tree) Tj
T*
(   sub_dir.mkdir\(parents=True, exist_ok=True\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 2. Path components introspection) Tj
T*
(   target_file = sub_dir / "app_settings.json") Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Path Object: {target_file}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"File Name: {target_file.name}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"File Stem: {target_file.stem}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"File Suffix: {target_file.suffix}"\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"Parent Dir: {target_file.parent}"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 3. Fluent Read/Write methods \(no open/close boilerplate!\)) Tj
T*
(   target_file.write_text\('{"env": "production", "debug": false}', encoding="utf-8"\)) Tj
T*
(   content = target_file.read_text\(encoding="utf-8"\)) Tj
T*
ET

