12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Object Serialization using the pickle Module. Detail pickle.dump\(\), pickle.load\(\), the pickle protocol versions, and security risks of unpickling untrusted data.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Basis of Pickling:) Tj
T*
(   Pickling is Python's native binary serialization format that converts an arbitrary in-memory) Tj
T*
(   Python object hierarchy into an architecture-independent byte stream.) Tj
T*
(   - Serialization \(Pickling\): pickle.dump\(obj, file\) or pickle.dumps\(obj\) -> bytes.) Tj
T*
(   - Deserialization \(Unpickling\): pickle.load\(file\) or pickle.loads\(bytes\) -> obj.) Tj
T*
(   - Protocol Versions: Python 3.8+ defaults to Pickle Protocol 5 \(PEP 574\), supporting) Tj
T*
(   out-of-band data buffers for massive NumPy/machine learning arrays.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Critical Security Vulnerability of Pickle \(Remote Code Execution\):) Tj
T*
(   Pickle is NOT secure against malicious data!) Tj
T*
(   When an object is unpickled, Python invokes the object's __reduce__\(\) method. An attacker can) Tj
T*
(   craft a malicious pickle payload whose __reduce__\(\) method instructs the PVM to call) Tj
T*
(   os.system\('malicious_command'\), executing arbitrary code on the victim machine with full user) Tj
T*
(   privileges!) Tj
T*
(   Industry Rule: NEVER unpickle untrusted data received over a network or from unauthenticated) Tj
T*
(   users. Use JSON or Protocol Buffers instead.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Implementation & Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   import pickle) Tj
T*
(   import os) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   class NeuralNetConfig:) Tj
T*
(   def __init__\(self, layers: list, learning_rate: float\):) Tj
T*
(   self.layers = layers) Tj
T*
(   self.learning_rate = learning_rate) Tj
T*
(   self.weights = {"W1": [0.25, -0.1], "W2": [0.88, 0.45]}) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   def __repr__\(self\):) Tj
T*
(   return f"NeuralNetConfig\(layers={self.layers}, lr={self.learning_rate}\)") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
