12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Exception Handling Architecture in Python: try, except, else, finally, and the Built-in Exception Hierarchy \(BaseException vs Exception\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Foundations of Exception Handling:) Tj
T*
(   Exceptions are runtime events that disrupt normal program instruction flow when errors occur.) Tj
T*
(   Python's structured exception handling consists of four complementary clauses:) Tj
T*
(   1. try: Encloses code that could potentially encounter a runtime exception.) Tj
T*
(   2. except: Intercepts and handles matching exceptions, preventing process termination.) Tj
T*
(   3. else: Executes if and only if the try block completes successfully with zero exceptions!) Tj
T*
(   4. finally: Guaranteed execution block; runs under all circumstances \(normal completion, caught) Tj
T*
(   exceptions, uncaught exceptions, and early return statements\). Used for resource deallocation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Built-in Exception Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   - BaseException: The root ancestor of all exceptions.) Tj
T*
(   - KeyboardInterrupt: Raised when user presses Ctrl+C.) Tj
T*
(   - SystemExit: Raised by sys.exit\(\).) Tj
T*
(   - GeneratorExit: Raised when generator closes.) Tj
T*
(   - Exception: The base class for all non-system-exiting application exceptions!) Tj
T*
(   - ArithmeticError \(ZeroDivisionError, OverflowError\)) Tj
T*
(   - LookupError \(IndexError, KeyError\)) Tj
T*
(   - OSError \(FileNotFoundError, PermissionError, TimeoutError\)) Tj
T*
(   - ValueError, TypeError, AttributeError.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   def robust_division_service\(numerator, denominator\):) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"\\n--- Attempting: {numerator} / {denominator} ---"\)) Tj
T*
ET

