12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Exception Raising, Exception Chaining \(raise ... from ...\), and Custom User-Defined Exception Classes in Python with domain models.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Raising and Re-Raising Exceptions:) Tj
T*
(   The raise statement triggers an exception immediately: raise ValueError\('Message'\).) Tj
T*
(   Writing a bare raise inside an except block re-raises the active exception currently being) Tj
T*
(   handled, preserving the original traceback.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Exception Chaining \(PEP 3134\):) Tj
T*
(   When translating low-level technical errors \(e.g., sqlite3.OperationalError\) into high-level) Tj
T*
(   business domain exceptions \(e.g., DatabaseQueryFailed\):) Tj
T*
(   Syntax: raise CustomException\('Context'\) from original_error) Tj
T*
(   - Sets the __cause__ attribute on the new exception, explicitly linking both errors in the) Tj
T*
(   traceback.) Tj
T*
(   - If from None is used \(raise CustomException\(...\) from None\), it suppresses the context,) Tj
T*
(   hiding sensitive underlying tracebacks.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. User-Defined Exception Hierarchy:) Tj
T*
(   Best practice: Define a base domain exception inheriting from Exception, then derive) Tj
T*
(   specialized subclasses.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Python Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   class BankingDomainError\(Exception\):) Tj
T*
(   """Root domain exception.""") Tj
T*
ET

