(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Capturing Groups, Non-Capturing Groups, Named Groups, and Backreferences in Python Regular Expressions with practical code examples.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Grouping Syntax & Semantics:) Tj
T*
(   - Capturing Groups \(pattern\): Groups subpatterns together and captures the matched substring) Tj
T*
(   into numbered slots \(group\(1\), group\(2\)\).) Tj
T*
(   - Non-Capturing Groups \(?:pattern\): Groups subpatterns for quantifiers or alternations WITHOUT) Tj
T*
(   storing them in memory or capturing slots. Faster and saves memory.) Tj
T*
(   - Named Capturing Groups \(?P<name>pattern\): Assigns an explicit symbolic name to the group,) Tj
T*
(   accessible via match.group\('name'\) or match.groupdict\(\).) Tj
T*
(   - Backreferences \(\\1, \\2 or \(?P=name\)\): Matches the exact same text that was captured earlier) Tj
T*
(   by that group within the same regular expression!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Practical Use Case: HTML Tag Balancing & Duplicate Word Detection:) Tj
T*
(   Detecting duplicate consecutive words: r'\\b\(\\w+\)\\s+\\1\\b' matches 'the the'.) Tj
T*
(   Matching balanced HTML tags: r'<\(?P<tag>\\w+\)>.*?</\(?P=tag\)>'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Code Demonstration:) Tj
T*
(   import re) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # 1. Named Capturing Groups for URL Parsing) Tj
T*
